            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Sets every value within lambda of zero to 0, and moves everything outside that band towards zero by lambda.\n\nLike hardshrink it silences a band around zero, but the survivors are shrunk rather than passed whole, so the output starts from 0 at the edge of the band and grows smoothly - no jump. That is the usual way to remove small noise from a signal (it is the \"soft thresholding\" of wavelet denoising): the step in hardshrink is itself a kind of noise.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFlat at 0 from -lambda to lambda. Outside, two lines parallel to the input but lambda closer to zero: with lambda 0.5, 2 gives 1.5 and -2 gives -1.5. Two corners, no jumps.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.softshrink\nNo arguments; default lambda 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nlambda (drag float on the node, and an inlet):\nThe half-width of the dead zone, and how much every survivor is shrunk. Default 0.5. It must not be negative: the console reports \"lambda must be greater or equal to 0\" and nothing is sent. It does not trigger the node: the next tensor uses it. In the demo the metro re-sends the ramp ten times a second, so the curve follows the slider.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.hardshrink keeps the survivors at full size, with a jump at the edges. t.nn.tanhshrink is a dead zone with no corners. noise_gate does a similar job on a stream of numbers.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 840,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
